**Partnership** (Indian Partnership Act, 1932): a relation between persons who agree to share the profits of a business carried on by all of them or by any of them acting for all.

**Features:** two or more persons (maximum 50, under the Companies Act, 2013 and its rules); an agreement, oral or written; a lawful business; sharing of profits; mutual agency; unlimited liability. Registration of a firm is optional.

**Partnership deed:** the written agreement. It usually covers the names of the firm and partners, nature and duration of business, capitals, profit-sharing ratio, interest on capital, drawings and loans, salaries and commission, accounting rules, and rules for admission, retirement, death, goodwill, disputes and dissolution.

**When the deed is silent (the Act applies):**
- profits and losses shared **equally**;
- **no** interest on capital; **no** interest on drawings;
- **no** salary or commission to any partner;
- interest on a partner's **loan** at **6% per annum**, a charge against profit.

**Charges** (Profit and Loss Account): interest on a partner's loan, rent paid to a partner, salaries of employees. Allowed even in a loss.

**Appropriations** (Profit and Loss Appropriation Account): interest on capital, salary and commission to partners, transfer to reserve, shares of profit. Allowed only out of profit.

**Format:** credit net profit and interest on drawings; debit interest on capital, salary, commission and reserve; the balance (**divisible profit**) is credited to partners in the profit-sharing ratio.

**Interest on capital:**
- on opening capital for the year; on additional capital from its date; reduced for capital withdrawn from its date;
- deed silent: none;
- appropriation, enough profit: full interest;
- appropriation, profit short: the whole profit is shared in the **ratio of interest due**;
- appropriation, loss: no interest;
- charge: full interest debited to the Profit and Loss Account, even if it causes a loss.
